1
00:00:15,113 --> 00:00:29,501
[SPEAKER_01]: So, very, very basic question is, so, what people might ask, and people do ask, you know, what the Buddhist teaching can offer more than other religions and other philosophies?

2
00:00:29,721 --> 00:00:30,581
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what is the difference?

3
00:00:30,702 --> 00:00:38,306
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, because they all teach some kind of release from suffering and happy life, meaningful life.

4
00:00:38,826 --> 00:00:44,169
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what is there, what is good, what is there a good reason to follow Buddhist teaching instead of others?

5
00:00:45,592 --> 00:00:50,195
[SPEAKER_05]: Well, do other religions teach freedom from suffering?

6
00:00:50,235 --> 00:00:52,897
[SPEAKER_01]: They would say yes, like happiness in the heavens.

7
00:00:53,638 --> 00:00:54,779
[SPEAKER_05]: Right, okay.

8
00:00:56,020 --> 00:00:57,020
[SPEAKER_05]: So, I think that's the difference.

9
00:00:57,341 --> 00:01:03,285
[SPEAKER_05]: The Buddhist teaching offers you freedom from suffering here and now, without having to wait for the heavens.

10
00:01:04,466 --> 00:01:09,730
[SPEAKER_05]: And, as the Buddha himself said, it's directly verifiable by those who make the effort.

11
00:01:10,050 --> 00:01:11,251
[SPEAKER_05]: So, that's kind of the difference.

12
00:01:11,621 --> 00:01:14,262
[SPEAKER_05]: And actually, yeah, I would... you could probably sum up like that.

13
00:01:14,822 --> 00:01:34,408
[SPEAKER_05]: That's also why, for me as well, I mean, I was in the beginning reading every religion when I was interested, because I was trying to learn, but that was something that I could not come to terms with, was the idea of... you reach a certain point where you're just left with faith, and you just have to have faith now, and things will work out.

14
00:01:34,688 --> 00:01:36,068
[SPEAKER_05]: And that was not good enough for me.

15
00:01:36,208 --> 00:01:41,370
[SPEAKER_05]: So, I wanted basically something that would not require faith as an endgame,

16
00:01:41,870 --> 00:01:44,792
[SPEAKER_05]: And something that you can verify, and then you don't have to do anything anymore.

17
00:01:45,932 --> 00:01:50,115
[SPEAKER_05]: And the only religion... religion that I found that was the Buddhist teaching.

18
00:01:50,915 --> 00:01:59,160
[SPEAKER_01]: Some religions, like Daoism, or maybe in Christianity, they also require some developing of wisdom, so it's not just faith related.

19
00:01:59,460 --> 00:02:01,761
[SPEAKER_05]: Well, it is all within faith and faith-based.

20
00:02:01,881 --> 00:02:06,564
[SPEAKER_05]: Like, you develop wisdom up to the point of belief in God, but you do not question that.

21
00:02:07,384 --> 00:02:08,425
[SPEAKER_05]: And, on top of it,

22
00:02:10,025 --> 00:02:11,326
[SPEAKER_05]: Doesn't matter what practice you do.

23
00:02:11,466 --> 00:02:19,469
[SPEAKER_05]: Even the most austere Christian monks, it's still... that practice is secondary to the will of God, who will then free you from suffering.

24
00:02:20,349 --> 00:02:25,071
[SPEAKER_05]: And, eh... if anybody is sort of self-transparent, authentic, that's not a good deal.

25
00:02:25,251 --> 00:02:28,673
[SPEAKER_05]: You don't want any other being to have a say in your well-being.

26
00:02:29,873 --> 00:02:33,875
[SPEAKER_05]: And that's what the Buddha himself sort of insisted on.

27
00:02:33,895 --> 00:02:36,836
[SPEAKER_05]: He said the gods themselves are subject to same things you are subjected to.

28
00:02:37,743 --> 00:02:38,744
[SPEAKER_05]: And that's the main difference.

29
00:02:38,844 --> 00:02:46,787
[SPEAKER_05]: So, you make it... you make the effort yourself for freedom and there is no intermediary or some medium in between.

30
00:02:49,168 --> 00:02:54,090
[SPEAKER_01]: So, is there... there's no faith in Buddhism or is also element of faith there?

31
00:02:54,110 --> 00:02:58,272
[SPEAKER_05]: The element of faith, as I said many times before, it's the faith you would have in a doctor.

32
00:02:59,293 --> 00:03:06,696
[SPEAKER_05]: So, you know you're ill, you... you're seeking for the medicine, so you have faith that this doctor might help you or that doctor might help you.

33
00:03:07,026 --> 00:03:13,507
[SPEAKER_05]: To the extent that on faith you take the medicine, but not for the sake of taking the medicine, or for the sake of faith.

34
00:03:14,928 --> 00:03:16,088
[SPEAKER_05]: You take it for the sake of health.

35
00:03:16,208 --> 00:03:21,669
[SPEAKER_05]: So, if you're not curing as a result of the medicine, then you don't have to keep that faith.

36
00:03:23,889 --> 00:03:25,670
[SPEAKER_01]: But one has to have faith in the Buddha's teaching.

37
00:03:26,470 --> 00:03:28,690
[SPEAKER_05]: Well, one has to have... one has to apply the medicine.

38
00:03:29,250 --> 00:03:33,811
[SPEAKER_05]: So, one has to have faith that the medicine might work, because otherwise, why would you apply it in the first place?

39
00:03:38,805 --> 00:03:42,448
[SPEAKER_05]: So, the purpose of faith, then, it's not just don't question it.

40
00:03:42,548 --> 00:03:49,995
[SPEAKER_05]: It's more like, yeah, this person or this teaching might be able to help me or help my existential situation.

41
00:03:50,055 --> 00:03:54,198
[SPEAKER_05]: So, thus, I will do what the medicine prescribes.

42
00:03:54,278 --> 00:03:55,279
[SPEAKER_05]: I will keep the precepts.

43
00:03:55,339 --> 00:03:56,180
[SPEAKER_05]: I will be virtuous.

44
00:03:56,240 --> 00:04:02,365
[SPEAKER_05]: I will reflect on my actions, develop transparency towards my intentions and see if it works.

45
00:04:03,846 --> 00:04:05,568
[SPEAKER_05]: So, that's the extent of faith.

46
00:04:07,077 --> 00:04:15,583
[SPEAKER_01]: So, since you don't have a God, or worshipping the God in Buddhist teaching, what is then the good reason to practice virtue?

47
00:04:15,923 --> 00:04:22,788
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, like all the religions, they have some kind of commandments, rules, emphasizing good behavior for the sake of heaven, then... Yeah.

48
00:04:23,348 --> 00:04:27,491
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah, well, the reason is the most direct reason, which is basically taming of your mind.

49
00:04:28,492 --> 00:04:35,317
[SPEAKER_05]: Because if you start practicing Buddhism, or even learning about it, you'll see that the suffering, possibility of suffering,

50
00:04:35,922 --> 00:04:39,745
[SPEAKER_05]: Any misfortune in the world that you might experience does not come from the world.

51
00:04:40,525 --> 00:04:41,386
[SPEAKER_05]: It comes from your mind.

52
00:04:43,647 --> 00:04:46,709
[SPEAKER_05]: And the reason why it comes from your mind is because that mind is not trained.

53
00:04:47,890 --> 00:04:52,633
[SPEAKER_05]: And you cannot train the mind unless you train your body first and actions in virtue.

54
00:04:53,053 --> 00:04:54,074
[SPEAKER_05]: As simple as that.

55
00:04:55,495 --> 00:04:59,998
[SPEAKER_05]: And when you have developed virtue and have trained the mind, then you cannot suffer amidst the suffering.

56
00:05:06,139 --> 00:05:12,561
[SPEAKER_01]: Is there some kind of thing, as they would say, original sin, is something like that applied to Buddhism?

57
00:05:13,622 --> 00:05:13,842
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

58
00:05:14,842 --> 00:05:17,583
[SPEAKER_01]: But there would be that the fan doesn't have its own beginning.

59
00:05:17,743 --> 00:05:18,624
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah, yeah.

60
00:05:19,664 --> 00:05:23,406
[SPEAKER_01]: So, but there's also teaching about five and then also eight precepts.

61
00:05:23,466 --> 00:05:31,989
[SPEAKER_01]: That's something that Vāsikas are going to take, how to say, commitment to those rules.

62
00:05:32,689 --> 00:05:34,750
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what's

63
00:05:36,018 --> 00:05:40,681
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that we don't present it as something... or this is... if you want to be Buddhist, you have to have five or eight precepts.

64
00:05:41,401 --> 00:05:47,625
[SPEAKER_01]: So, why, for example, should be following five precepts and also eight?

65
00:05:48,605 --> 00:05:51,067
[SPEAKER_05]: It just depends how far you want to go in practice.

66
00:05:51,307 --> 00:06:00,332
[SPEAKER_05]: So, I guess, for somebody who hasn't followed any precepts, five precepts is a good place to start, because that's how you will learn.

67
00:06:00,592 --> 00:06:02,413
[SPEAKER_05]: See where the sensory strain lies.

68
00:06:04,011 --> 00:06:09,694
[SPEAKER_05]: And the point of it, then, if you want to take it further, you increase the restraint, in terms of the eight precepts.

69
00:06:11,354 --> 00:06:13,295
[SPEAKER_05]: But it's all for the purpose of training.

70
00:06:13,375 --> 00:06:17,677
[SPEAKER_05]: Because, yes, you will often have people take them on a religious level.

71
00:06:18,037 --> 00:06:20,178
[SPEAKER_05]: Like, I keep the precepts, therefore I'm Buddhist.

72
00:06:21,739 --> 00:06:24,000
[SPEAKER_05]: Or, I'm born Buddhist, therefore I keep the precepts.

73
00:06:24,360 --> 00:06:27,081
[SPEAKER_05]: I mean, it's good, but it will not result in wisdom.

74
00:06:27,202 --> 00:06:30,103
[SPEAKER_02]: Could we quickly go through what these precepts are?

75
00:06:30,928 --> 00:06:53,209
[SPEAKER_05]: So, the five precepts, the first one is no killing, no stealing, no misconduct in sensual pleasures, we can define that, no lying, and no taking of intoxicants, alcohol, drugs, anything that would sort of contribute towards laxness of the mind.

76
00:06:55,833 --> 00:07:02,954
[SPEAKER_05]: Misconduct in sexual and sensual pleasures means, basically, although a person is not celibate, they are restrained.

77
00:07:03,374 --> 00:07:14,876
[SPEAKER_05]: So, they don't overdo it, they don't allow their life to revolve around pleasures and gaining pleasures and seeking, but exercising some sort of moderation and sense restraint.

78
00:07:15,577 --> 00:07:23,518
[SPEAKER_05]: So, yeah, even that in itself, just five precepts, if taken rightly, can result in taming your mind to some degree, because

79
00:07:25,675 --> 00:07:26,716
[SPEAKER_05]: That's where it starts, really.

80
00:07:27,377 --> 00:07:29,880
[SPEAKER_05]: The bodily actions and choices we make on account of them.

81
00:07:31,742 --> 00:07:33,784
[SPEAKER_05]: You're trying to train an animal.

82
00:07:34,444 --> 00:07:42,192
[SPEAKER_05]: If there is no discipline, signal, patience, waiting, enduring, just let it eat whenever it wants, it's not going to listen to you.

83
00:07:42,312 --> 00:07:42,953
[SPEAKER_05]: Same with the mind.

84
00:07:43,850 --> 00:07:57,459
[SPEAKER_05]: If you act whenever there is a desire or a thought towards it, and no restraint, even fear of enduring that little discomfort of saying no to yourself, then the mind will keep doing that, and it will be controlling you.

85
00:07:57,479 --> 00:08:00,681
[SPEAKER_05]: So, that's what needs to be developed.

86
00:08:00,701 --> 00:08:06,825
[SPEAKER_05]: It's not going to happen magically, like, miraculously, it will just stop on its own, because that's not what it does.

87
00:08:09,296 --> 00:08:11,597
[SPEAKER_02]: These are the five, and then you also have eight and ten.

88
00:08:11,837 --> 00:08:12,017
[SPEAKER_02]: Yes.

89
00:08:12,517 --> 00:08:14,438
[SPEAKER_05]: So, the eighth precept is the third precept.

90
00:08:14,478 --> 00:08:18,800
[SPEAKER_05]: The misconduct in spirituality becomes full celibacy.

91
00:08:18,980 --> 00:08:22,542
[SPEAKER_05]: And then, you add another precept, like, you don't eat after the noon.

92
00:08:23,722 --> 00:08:26,923
[SPEAKER_05]: So, like, slightly increased moderation in eating.

93
00:08:28,730 --> 00:08:48,946
[SPEAKER_05]: Then, don't... like sleeping on high and luxurious places, which is basically more like, again, on a practical level, don't just indulge in sleep, you know, have a slightly less comfortable bed, that will prevent you from laying in it for 12 hours and stuff like that, because that all just contributes towards mental and physical sluggishness.

94
00:08:49,742 --> 00:08:50,983
[SPEAKER_05]: And the beautification.

95
00:08:51,383 --> 00:08:56,728
[SPEAKER_05]: So, again, being overly concerned about your sensual appearance.

96
00:08:56,908 --> 00:09:03,834
[SPEAKER_05]: So, beauty, perfumes... Another added precept to the five is that entertainment.

97
00:09:04,914 --> 00:09:08,778
[SPEAKER_05]: So, which is shows, dances, music, stuff like that.

98
00:09:09,698 --> 00:09:17,184
[SPEAKER_05]: Ten precepts is... that's like when a person kind of joins the order as a novice, as a beginner.

99
00:09:17,785 --> 00:09:19,026
[SPEAKER_05]: So, then you give up money.

100
00:09:19,730 --> 00:09:24,633
[SPEAKER_05]: Gold, silver, valuables, like, that you can trade with money, basically.

101
00:09:25,333 --> 00:09:32,837
[SPEAKER_05]: You don't handle it, so you're kind of... not as independent, I suppose, in that sense.

102
00:09:33,118 --> 00:09:37,280
[SPEAKER_05]: Just creates even a greater sense of urgency.

103
00:09:37,580 --> 00:09:39,641
[SPEAKER_04]: And what's the... That's... That's it, yeah.

104
00:09:39,661 --> 00:09:39,901
[SPEAKER_04]: That's...

105
00:09:39,921 --> 00:09:40,942
[SPEAKER_04]: It's broken into two, isn't it?

106
00:09:40,962 --> 00:09:42,203
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, one is broken into two, yeah.

107
00:09:42,563 --> 00:09:42,823
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

108
00:09:42,843 --> 00:09:46,685
[SPEAKER_01]: So we can't... Entertainment and beautification separate, really.

109
00:09:47,225 --> 00:09:47,946
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah, something like that.

110
00:09:48,840 --> 00:09:54,366
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah, one of the eight is separated into two, so you get nine, and then you give up the money as ten.

111
00:09:54,466 --> 00:09:55,787
[SPEAKER_05]: Nine doesn't exist.

112
00:09:57,309 --> 00:10:10,642
[SPEAKER_02]: So, the main purpose behind these precepts is basically to tame your mind, to calm your mind enough to then be able to attain a certain

113
00:10:11,644 --> 00:10:12,044
[SPEAKER_05]: No.

114
00:10:12,404 --> 00:10:13,324
[SPEAKER_05]: Well, yeah, I suppose, yeah.

115
00:10:13,644 --> 00:10:14,925
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah, to see things clearer.

116
00:10:15,205 --> 00:10:20,106
[SPEAKER_05]: Like, as I said, even if you don't necessarily... Hey, drama.

117
00:10:24,867 --> 00:10:38,490
[SPEAKER_05]: Even if you don't have some, you know, lofty goals in terms of practice, just being able to say no to yourself and endure your own state of mind, that's an accomplishment.

118
00:10:38,550 --> 00:10:40,130
[SPEAKER_05]: The life will be much better.

119
00:10:41,402 --> 00:10:43,623
[SPEAKER_05]: Because, yeah, the mind doesn't listen to you, you know.

120
00:10:43,643 --> 00:10:46,984
[SPEAKER_05]: It's nice when it's in a good mood, but it's not nice when it's not in a good mood.

121
00:10:47,024 --> 00:10:47,824
[SPEAKER_05]: But you're paired with it.

122
00:10:47,844 --> 00:10:48,524
[SPEAKER_05]: You have to endure it.

123
00:10:49,425 --> 00:10:54,846
[SPEAKER_05]: The most people's concerns revolve around trying to change the mind, or control the mind, and make it obey them.

124
00:10:55,006 --> 00:10:57,067
[SPEAKER_05]: But that's not the problem.

125
00:10:57,167 --> 00:11:03,969
[SPEAKER_05]: The problem is simply that you want mind to be a certain way, and then you resist it when it's not that way.

126
00:11:04,029 --> 00:11:04,589
[SPEAKER_05]: So, it's on you.

127
00:11:04,950 --> 00:11:05,890
[SPEAKER_05]: If you stop resisting it,

128
00:11:06,758 --> 00:11:11,279
[SPEAKER_05]: If you learn how to be patient and endure it, nothing that the mind can throw at you can bother you.

129
00:11:12,599 --> 00:11:17,140
[SPEAKER_05]: So that's that's the whole idea of like, you know, learning also how to disown it correctly.

130
00:11:18,521 --> 00:11:23,722
[SPEAKER_05]: Because the idea of that ownership that the Buddha often talked about, it's the idea of control.

131
00:11:25,222 --> 00:11:27,162
[SPEAKER_05]: You want to control things that are not in your control.

132
00:11:28,403 --> 00:11:31,723
[SPEAKER_05]: And you don't want to let go of the idea that they are in your control.

133
00:11:31,904 --> 00:11:34,064
[SPEAKER_05]: So that that's like you suffer twice as much.

134
00:11:35,534 --> 00:11:38,255
[SPEAKER_05]: That doesn't mean now you just let go of everything, just surrender.

135
00:11:38,475 --> 00:11:38,695
[SPEAKER_05]: No.

136
00:11:38,955 --> 00:11:40,175
[SPEAKER_05]: Don't let go of the precepts.

137
00:11:40,875 --> 00:11:46,436
[SPEAKER_05]: Don't let go of taming that mind, but don't confuse it as something that belongs to you, because it doesn't.

138
00:11:47,436 --> 00:11:50,877
[SPEAKER_02]: And what you're talking about now is non-self, isn't it?

139
00:11:51,237 --> 00:11:51,877
[SPEAKER_05]: Exactly, yeah.

140
00:11:51,897 --> 00:11:53,178
[SPEAKER_02]: It's just one of the big concepts.

141
00:11:53,258 --> 00:12:04,640
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah, that's... Again, even the not... That's why we wouldn't necessarily, or quite often, would not even talk about, oh, is there self, is there non-self, and provide answers to that, because it's not the point.

142
00:12:05,652 --> 00:12:32,432
[SPEAKER_05]: Of answering an intellectual query, it's more like, if you train correctly, tame your mind, practice sense restraint, your experience will start approximating the experience of non-self, and eventually it will be the experience of non-self, the non-control, not like negation of everything, nothing is my problem, or like, no, I'm not trying to control things that I know are not in my control, nor do I suffer on account of them when they change, because you suffer is when you still try to control them.

143
00:12:33,334 --> 00:12:35,776
[SPEAKER_03]: How do you know you're in non-self?

144
00:12:36,236 --> 00:12:36,476
[SPEAKER_03]: Sorry?

145
00:12:36,676 --> 00:12:44,342
[SPEAKER_03]: How do you actually know you're there, in a free will, non-self?

146
00:12:44,582 --> 00:12:49,886
[SPEAKER_05]: By, I suppose, yeah, by fulfilling the training and not being able to suffer on account of your mind changing.

147
00:12:51,107 --> 00:12:54,929
[SPEAKER_05]: So it doesn't matter how your mind changes, it doesn't take you with it.

148
00:12:56,370 --> 00:12:58,952
[SPEAKER_05]: And then you know you're not affected anymore.

149
00:12:58,992 --> 00:13:00,073
[SPEAKER_05]: And that's what non-self is.

150
00:13:02,936 --> 00:13:18,067
[SPEAKER_05]: When sensual desire doesn't move you, when disagreeable things don't overwhelm you and cause ill will, when nothing to do does not scare you, bore you and fill you with anxiety, when you're at ease with your own mind and whichever state it's in.

151
00:13:21,470 --> 00:13:28,374
[SPEAKER_02]: I mean, correct me if I'm wrong, I believe Buddha never talked or didn't speak much of happiness.

152
00:13:28,914 --> 00:13:32,456
[SPEAKER_02]: Rather, he spoke of freedom from suffering.

153
00:13:32,476 --> 00:13:32,676
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

154
00:13:32,696 --> 00:13:34,016
[SPEAKER_02]: This is what you're saying now, isn't it?

155
00:13:34,737 --> 00:13:34,957
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah.

156
00:13:35,957 --> 00:13:40,560
[SPEAKER_05]: Basically, happiness comes when you undo everything that makes you suffer.

157
00:13:41,900 --> 00:13:48,904
[SPEAKER_05]: But if you start talking about happiness, people, they just want to go straight into happiness without undoing everything that makes them suffer.

158
00:13:48,924 --> 00:13:49,504
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah, yeah.

159
00:13:50,385 --> 00:13:52,787
[SPEAKER_05]: So, that's why you avoided it in that way.

160
00:13:55,389 --> 00:13:57,831
[SPEAKER_01]: It seems in Buddhism also can happen, like, two extremes.

161
00:13:57,891 --> 00:14:03,275
[SPEAKER_01]: You have the ones who take the rules quite religiously, like five precepts and so on.

162
00:14:03,535 --> 00:14:05,037
[SPEAKER_01]: That's especially in Asian countries.

163
00:14:05,737 --> 00:14:14,144
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you have people who, maybe in the West, who just think Buddhism is just about calming the mind and meditating, but they don't purify the virtue.

164
00:14:14,164 --> 00:14:14,564
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah.

165
00:14:14,905 --> 00:14:17,727
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the Buddhists didn't go to these two extremes.

166
00:14:18,249 --> 00:14:25,334
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah, that's why, like, if you do it for the reasons of practice and mind development, you will find the right balance between the two.

167
00:14:26,535 --> 00:14:27,475
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah, that's something you...

168
00:14:27,495 --> 00:14:33,400
[SPEAKER_05]: I mean, you can just teach people, but it's up to every individual to really clarify that and do it rightly.

169
00:14:33,420 --> 00:14:33,620
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah.

170
00:14:37,102 --> 00:14:46,149
[SPEAKER_01]: Janice advised, it seems that lay people in Buddhistan, they were following five precepts, and on Uposatha, eight precepts.

171
00:14:47,095 --> 00:14:53,818
[SPEAKER_01]: That's kind of... it's interesting to see this... how the Buddha was emphasizing that one.

172
00:14:54,659 --> 00:14:55,820
[SPEAKER_01]: At least once a week, let's say.

173
00:14:56,360 --> 00:14:59,121
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's one thing we try to emphasize in the Pāsaka group.

174
00:15:01,642 --> 00:15:03,704
[SPEAKER_01]: Maybe you can say something more about eight precepts.

