00:38:23,712 --> 00:38:27,734
[SPEAKER_05]: If you understood Four Noble Truths, basically, you don't need anyone's help anymore.

551
00:38:27,774 --> 00:38:28,775
[SPEAKER_05]: You know exactly what to do.

552
00:38:30,154 --> 00:38:32,935
[SPEAKER_05]: You know exactly where the cause of suffering is rooted.

553
00:38:33,616 --> 00:38:37,158
[SPEAKER_05]: And, you know, the answer is craving of your own mind, not the world.

554
00:38:37,258 --> 00:38:41,920
[SPEAKER_05]: But until that's clear to you, you haven't seen the Four Noble Truths.

555
00:38:41,940 --> 00:38:44,061
[SPEAKER_01]: So could you maybe first say what the Four Noble Truths are?

556
00:38:44,081 --> 00:38:44,201
[SPEAKER_01]: Right.

557
00:38:44,221 --> 00:38:53,005
[SPEAKER_05]: The Four Noble Truths are suffering, the origin of suffering, the cessation of suffering, and the path that would lead to that cessation.

558
00:38:53,126 --> 00:38:57,748
[SPEAKER_05]: But it's really just one truth of understanding where suffering is.

559
00:38:59,196 --> 00:39:08,164
[SPEAKER_05]: Because most minds would think suffering is in things, the world, others, this or that.

560
00:39:09,224 --> 00:39:11,006
[SPEAKER_05]: But there is only one cause for suffering.

561
00:39:11,266 --> 00:39:16,010
[SPEAKER_05]: So, if the suffering is present in your mind, it's because that cause is present in your mind.

562
00:39:16,671 --> 00:39:18,532
[SPEAKER_05]: And that cause is craving.

563
00:39:18,572 --> 00:39:20,274
[SPEAKER_03]: But can't suffering be healthy?

564
00:39:20,954 --> 00:39:21,194
[SPEAKER_03]: Sorry?

565
00:39:21,595 --> 00:39:23,056
[SPEAKER_03]: Can't suffering be healthy?

566
00:39:23,376 --> 00:39:26,359
[SPEAKER_03]: If I'm suffering, I will strive to be a better person.

567
00:39:27,367 --> 00:39:29,088
[SPEAKER_03]: Would be a very simple analysis.

568
00:39:29,149 --> 00:39:29,909
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah, yeah.

569
00:39:30,450 --> 00:39:34,133
[SPEAKER_05]: So, as I said, that would be the suffering of taking the medicine that's unpleasant.

570
00:39:34,573 --> 00:39:35,294
[SPEAKER_03]: No, no, no, no.

571
00:39:35,914 --> 00:39:40,378
[SPEAKER_03]: I'm suffering, therefore, I will go and do something about the suffering.

572
00:39:40,418 --> 00:39:42,159
[SPEAKER_03]: Not necessarily cause it.

573
00:39:42,239 --> 00:39:49,906
[SPEAKER_03]: If I, if I see suffering in the world, I will, therefore, go and

574
00:39:50,543 --> 00:39:54,564
[SPEAKER_03]: I should do something about it, instead of cease that suffering within myself.

575
00:39:54,644 --> 00:39:54,864
[SPEAKER_05]: Right.

576
00:39:55,284 --> 00:39:58,244
[SPEAKER_05]: No, but suffering in the world is not the suffering in yourself.

577
00:39:58,304 --> 00:39:59,145
[SPEAKER_05]: That's not the same thing.

578
00:40:00,365 --> 00:40:02,305
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, inside you're going to be suffering.

579
00:40:02,825 --> 00:40:07,386
[SPEAKER_05]: You can go and help the world and diminish world suffering, even if you don't suffer internally.

580
00:40:07,406 --> 00:40:08,706
[SPEAKER_05]: It has nothing to do with each other.

581
00:40:09,647 --> 00:40:11,527
[SPEAKER_05]: The internal suffering is what we're talking about.

582
00:40:11,567 --> 00:40:13,167
[SPEAKER_03]: But it's a motivating factor.

583
00:40:14,067 --> 00:40:14,327
[SPEAKER_05]: Well, no.

584
00:40:14,347 --> 00:40:16,408
[SPEAKER_05]: The motivating factor is the desire and you want to fulfill it.

585
00:40:16,428 --> 00:40:17,348
[SPEAKER_03]: It depends how you turn it.

586
00:40:19,302 --> 00:40:25,465
[SPEAKER_03]: Going back to my analysis of good and bad lives, it's also you've got good and bad suffering.

587
00:40:26,425 --> 00:40:28,926
[SPEAKER_05]: Again, we're talking about two different levels of suffering.

588
00:40:29,066 --> 00:40:36,569
[SPEAKER_05]: The suffering, the Four Noble Truths of Suffering, is the internal suffering, the internal dis-ease, uneasiness of the mind.

589
00:40:36,649 --> 00:40:39,290
[SPEAKER_05]: It's not the suffering of the world and that you might perceive in others.

590
00:40:41,171 --> 00:40:43,712
[SPEAKER_05]: So, what I'm saying is, the suffering you refer to,

591
00:40:44,513 --> 00:40:56,699
[SPEAKER_05]: It's not much really to do with what we're talking about, in a sense of, if you were to uproot the suffering inside of you, remove the craving, you can still help the world, as the Buddha himself did, and diminish their suffering.

592
00:40:56,739 --> 00:40:59,800
[SPEAKER_03]: For example, I'm suffering because I'm hungry.

593
00:41:00,460 --> 00:41:07,303
[SPEAKER_03]: Now, I know Buddha decided to starve himself, and then went into moderation, and then decided to eat a lot.

594
00:41:08,444 --> 00:41:10,725
[SPEAKER_03]: Now, have I got that wrong?

595
00:41:12,580 --> 00:41:19,964
[SPEAKER_03]: But because I'm hungry, I'm not going to stare for practice, attachment, non-attachment.

596
00:41:20,304 --> 00:41:23,746
[SPEAKER_03]: I will go and do something like go and get work, so I can feed myself.

597
00:41:23,786 --> 00:41:24,286
[SPEAKER_05]: Right, right.

598
00:41:24,706 --> 00:41:26,887
[SPEAKER_05]: So, well, again, that's two separate things.

599
00:41:27,588 --> 00:41:29,208
[SPEAKER_05]: Hunger is not the cause of suffering.

600
00:41:29,849 --> 00:41:33,270
[SPEAKER_05]: And that's... if you think that, it's because you haven't seen the Four Noble Truths.

601
00:41:33,410 --> 00:41:36,632
[SPEAKER_05]: And that was the insight that the Buddha saw that nobody else did.

602
00:41:37,662 --> 00:41:40,244
[SPEAKER_05]: Discomfort in the world is not the cause of suffering.

603
00:41:40,824 --> 00:41:42,306
[SPEAKER_05]: Hunger is not the cause of suffering.

604
00:41:42,766 --> 00:41:47,409
[SPEAKER_05]: Words of others, misfortune, being beaten, thrown, it's not the cause of suffering.

605
00:41:47,850 --> 00:41:52,053
[SPEAKER_05]: Craving, phenomenon of craving being present in your mind means presence of suffering.

606
00:41:52,073 --> 00:41:54,775
[SPEAKER_03]: I think I suffer a lot looking at the world as it is.

607
00:41:54,995 --> 00:41:56,935
[SPEAKER_00]: It's basically how you react to it.

608
00:41:56,955 --> 00:42:01,336
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, suffering exists and it's you how you react to it, you know.

609
00:42:01,677 --> 00:42:03,157
[SPEAKER_00]: If you let it affect you...

610
00:42:03,577 --> 00:42:08,278
[SPEAKER_03]: But I'm saying that you can let it affect you and you can do something positive.

611
00:42:08,298 --> 00:42:12,239
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, but you don't need to suffer because of suffering of others, you know.

612
00:42:12,399 --> 00:42:18,440
[SPEAKER_00]: You can know it's bad, but you don't let it affect you to a level where you would suffer on account of others.

613
00:42:18,480 --> 00:42:20,241
[SPEAKER_05]: And you can still go and help them.

614
00:42:20,441 --> 00:42:21,301
[SPEAKER_05]: It's not required.

615
00:42:22,617 --> 00:42:28,159
[SPEAKER_05]: If suffering is required to make you do something about it, again, it means you haven't understood the suffering.

616
00:42:33,661 --> 00:42:34,302
[SPEAKER_05]: So, hunger?

617
00:42:34,462 --> 00:42:42,885
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah, you can go and work and eat, but you don't need to suffer on account of it, which means you won't suffer on account of the pressure of work and having to earn.

618
00:42:43,505 --> 00:42:45,266
[SPEAKER_05]: Because what if I don't get enough food?

619
00:42:45,326 --> 00:42:47,807
[SPEAKER_05]: Well, hunger does not bother you in terms of suffering.

620
00:42:48,253 --> 00:42:50,113
[SPEAKER_05]: Death does not bother you in terms of suffering.

621
00:42:50,734 --> 00:42:53,494
[SPEAKER_05]: Because even death in itself is not the cause of suffering.

622
00:42:53,714 --> 00:42:55,075
[SPEAKER_05]: Illness is not the cause of suffering.

623
00:42:55,475 --> 00:42:56,615
[SPEAKER_05]: Taṇha means suffering.

624
00:42:56,735 --> 00:42:57,495
[SPEAKER_05]: First noble truth.

625
00:42:58,375 --> 00:43:01,276
[SPEAKER_05]: That's why it's like the universal truth that nobody else could see.

626
00:43:01,356 --> 00:43:03,637
[SPEAKER_05]: Because, yeah, it's... it's not self-evident.

627
00:43:03,817 --> 00:43:05,957
[SPEAKER_05]: If it were, every other person would become a Buddha.

628
00:43:07,318 --> 00:43:17,220
[SPEAKER_05]: So the Buddha was able to see, despite the pressure of the world, despite being on the verge of death himself, to starvation and everything else, that those things is not where suffering is truly rooted in.

629
00:43:18,530 --> 00:43:21,811
[SPEAKER_05]: It's the craving, like literally, internal craving of your mind.

630
00:43:22,491 --> 00:43:26,153
[SPEAKER_05]: Absence of that craving, regardless of circumstances, means peace.

631
00:43:27,253 --> 00:43:35,696
[SPEAKER_05]: To the point where he said, even if highway robbers grab you, pin you down, chop your limbs one by one, if you fulfill the teaching, you won't suffer on account of it.

632
00:43:35,816 --> 00:43:41,418
[SPEAKER_05]: Not that you will not experience the discomfort, but internal pain of the mind cannot be experienced ever again.

633
00:43:41,798 --> 00:43:43,699
[SPEAKER_05]: That's what he called unshakable liberation.

634
00:43:44,423 --> 00:43:47,909
[SPEAKER_05]: Not unshakable control of the world whereby nothing bad happens to you ever again.

635
00:43:48,169 --> 00:43:48,790
[SPEAKER_05]: Quite the opposite.

636
00:43:49,632 --> 00:43:53,739
[SPEAKER_05]: It's just unable to have the phenomenon of craving present in your mind.

637
00:43:54,460 --> 00:43:55,602
[SPEAKER_05]: Thus, unable to suffer.

638
00:43:59,540 --> 00:44:02,803
[SPEAKER_02]: That is the craving for out there, isn't it?

639
00:44:03,404 --> 00:44:06,767
[SPEAKER_02]: For things, phenomena, states...

640
00:44:07,508 --> 00:44:14,755
[SPEAKER_05]: Yes, it's kind of craving that's perpetuated through these actions that are rooted in desire, real will, and that's... we go back to the training of the precepts.

641
00:44:14,835 --> 00:44:18,078
[SPEAKER_05]: It's not like a commandment that you must obey.

642
00:44:18,098 --> 00:44:22,222
[SPEAKER_05]: It's just like... that's what... that's what keeps fueling the fire, the problem.

643
00:44:23,033 --> 00:44:25,135
[SPEAKER_05]: So, that's why you do it, until you uprooted it.

644
00:44:25,175 --> 00:44:28,378
[SPEAKER_05]: Then, afterwards, do what you like, but there'll be certain things you'll be unable to do.

645
00:44:29,079 --> 00:44:30,420
[SPEAKER_05]: Act out of craving and desire, yeah.

646
00:44:33,222 --> 00:44:47,055
[SPEAKER_01]: So, there's kind of hope in society that even they are occupied with their lay life, with family, jobs, living in society, they can still develop this mindfulness, wisdom and right view.

647
00:44:47,654 --> 00:44:47,854
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah.

648
00:44:48,835 --> 00:44:53,079
[SPEAKER_01]: I just kind of hope it's kind of worth to pursue this path and make an effort.

649
00:44:53,719 --> 00:45:07,410
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah, it's just, as I said, like, work is... comes down to keeping the precepts, sense restraint, being able to contain your desires to the point to see what this craving is and what you're supposed to see.

650
00:45:08,631 --> 00:45:09,993
[SPEAKER_05]: So, that doesn't mean that you can just

651
00:45:10,770 --> 00:45:14,013
[SPEAKER_05]: Go on and do stuff you used to do when you didn't want to practice.

652
00:45:14,073 --> 00:45:18,298
[SPEAKER_05]: Like, to some extent, your life will probably have to start tailoring what you want to achieve.

653
00:45:19,399 --> 00:45:25,345
[SPEAKER_05]: So, you can't be unrestrained, going with, you know, people with, you know, they would sort of take you in these wrong directions.

654
00:45:25,365 --> 00:45:31,231
[SPEAKER_05]: It's like, again, I want to train my dog, but I let it run with a wild pack of, you know, jackals and stuff.

655
00:45:31,251 --> 00:45:33,573
[SPEAKER_05]: Well, it's not going to become tame, it's going to become wilder.

656
00:45:34,607 --> 00:45:44,234
[SPEAKER_05]: So, to some extent, circumstances need to sort of be kept in check, but yeah, you don't need to go and live on top of a mountain or somehow, because that just might result in even more ignorance.

657
00:45:44,715 --> 00:45:52,040
[SPEAKER_01]: One thing we quite often emphasize is actually also, okay, seclusion from sensuality, so on, but also seclusion, physical seclusions.

658
00:45:54,442 --> 00:45:58,165
[SPEAKER_05]: Yes, yeah, of course, but again, I wouldn't worry about that much in the beginning.

659
00:45:59,286 --> 00:46:00,627
[SPEAKER_05]: That is useful later on, but

660
00:46:02,358 --> 00:46:09,139
[SPEAKER_05]: It's really the... keeping the precepts, those are the rules of training, let's say like that, the rules of engagement.

661
00:46:09,639 --> 00:46:17,741
[SPEAKER_05]: So, if the circumstances you're in keeps making you break the precepts, then you need to start revising a bit, just from that practical standpoint.

662
00:46:19,741 --> 00:46:27,343
[SPEAKER_05]: If the circumstance does not push you to break the precepts, then fine, keep precepts, keep practicing, read the suttas, reflect, and so on.

663
00:46:27,563 --> 00:46:28,763
[SPEAKER_05]: And then, yes, if you have time,

664
00:46:30,395 --> 00:46:37,180
[SPEAKER_05]: Spend more time in seclusion without distractions and so on, but that's not something... Oh, I must aim for that and that's where my practice will begin.

665
00:46:38,180 --> 00:46:41,483
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah, that's something people will have that view as well.

666
00:46:41,503 --> 00:46:59,595
[SPEAKER_04]: Maybe a different way of approaching this might be by asking you what would you say that from the perspective of, say, the more older or traditional or sutta buddhism, abhidhamma, no, sutta buddhism, would... what would, from this perspective,

667
00:47:00,075 --> 00:47:04,698
[SPEAKER_04]: Well, I think the first... Yeah, the first and obvious one is...

668
00:47:04,718 --> 00:47:07,780
[SPEAKER_04]: I think people think meditation leads to enlightenment.

669
00:47:08,061 --> 00:47:10,702
[SPEAKER_04]: As in, I don't have to do anything else.

670
00:47:10,722 --> 00:47:13,825
[SPEAKER_04]: I just need to learn how to meditate and enlightenment will happen to me.

671
00:47:30,771 --> 00:47:40,419
[SPEAKER_05]: And there are plenty of schools, you know, non-Buddhist and later Buddhists, they would be teaching that, but that's nowhere near the original instruction.

672
00:47:40,879 --> 00:47:46,323
[SPEAKER_05]: The second common thing would be Buddhism is for the world peace or something like that.

673
00:47:46,423 --> 00:47:47,904
[SPEAKER_05]: That was never Buddhist concern either.

674
00:47:50,026 --> 00:47:57,612
[SPEAKER_05]: It's just... Those are kind of... not necessarily something that would block people's progress, but it can certainly take them in the...

675
00:47:58,648 --> 00:48:00,949
[SPEAKER_05]: Different directions of learning, I suppose.

676
00:48:00,989 --> 00:48:02,670
[SPEAKER_05]: So, it's... it's fine.

677
00:48:02,710 --> 00:48:07,452
[SPEAKER_05]: People can choose to go wherever they want, but I think the most important thing is to at least... at least know the difference.

678
00:48:07,893 --> 00:48:15,977
[SPEAKER_05]: Like, knowing that... that what... what the kind of original instruction was, and what... what... what it was concerned with, let's say like that.

679
00:48:16,537 --> 00:48:19,238
[SPEAKER_05]: And then not confusing it for other... other things you come across.

680
00:48:22,360 --> 00:48:22,660
[SPEAKER_04]: Okay.

681
00:48:22,760 --> 00:48:23,981
[SPEAKER_04]: Related to this, maybe...

682
00:48:25,302 --> 00:48:26,783
My question would be...

683
00:48:27,543 --> 00:48:49,152
[SPEAKER_04]: I would assume that a lot of people in contemporary society would have issues with this emphasis on trying to somehow transcend desire, given the fact that desire, in one way or another, is basically the foundation on which the contemporary society is pretty much built.

684
00:48:49,393 --> 00:48:52,194
[SPEAKER_04]: And a lot of people do

685
00:48:54,984 --> 00:49:11,232
[SPEAKER_04]: Do subscribe to some version of this hedonistic or semi-hedonistic worldview, or if they don't, they would at least want to retain this idea of, well, I can still enjoy my desires without falling prey to them.

686
00:49:11,252 --> 00:49:21,177
[SPEAKER_04]: So, how would one bring closer to the contemporary person this notion of

687
00:49:22,000 --> 00:49:26,261
[SPEAKER_04]: Why desire is ultimately unfulfilling?

688
00:49:26,281 --> 00:49:30,383
[SPEAKER_04]: I'm actually here kind of hinting at that lovely video, a very short video that you made.

689
00:49:30,403 --> 00:49:37,705
[SPEAKER_04]: It was one of your first ones, I think, where you basically show why desire is unfulfilling, fundamentally unfulfilling.

690
00:49:38,085 --> 00:49:39,886
[SPEAKER_05]: No one wants centrality on that one, eh?

691
00:49:40,366 --> 00:49:41,406
[SPEAKER_05]: Precisely, yeah.

692
00:49:42,186 --> 00:49:47,948
[SPEAKER_05]: Well, again, even that video will make sense only to a person that kind of is already aware of its own situation.

693
00:49:48,008 --> 00:49:50,429
[SPEAKER_05]: If you just say to anybody randomly, they're not going to see it.

694
00:49:50,929 --> 00:49:51,129
[SPEAKER_05]: So,

695
00:49:51,992 --> 00:49:53,734
[SPEAKER_05]: You can't bring this.

696
00:49:54,675 --> 00:49:57,077
[SPEAKER_05]: The original instruction will never be mainstream.

697
00:49:57,277 --> 00:49:59,239
[SPEAKER_05]: I mean, it was mainstream when the Buddha was alive.

698
00:49:59,700 --> 00:50:00,060
[SPEAKER_05]: That's it.

699
00:50:01,521 --> 00:50:05,265
[SPEAKER_05]: After he passed away, it started to decline, because it goes against the whole world.

700
00:50:05,605 --> 00:50:06,806
[SPEAKER_05]: I mean, that's what he himself said.

701
00:50:06,946 --> 00:50:14,273
[SPEAKER_05]: The one who practices swims against the stream, going against the grain of saṃsara in the world that has its own direction.

702
00:50:14,374 --> 00:50:14,614
[SPEAKER_05]: So,

703
00:50:15,870 --> 00:50:20,654
[SPEAKER_05]: Going around preaching to people that desires are bad, it's just not... not really gonna work.

704
00:50:21,355 --> 00:50:35,947
[SPEAKER_05]: But those that are willing, that do recognize, that are dissatisfied with, you know, their mind or the kind of... the certain aspects of their situation, then they could perhaps hear it, or at least know the difference, and then maybe choose to do something about it.

705
00:50:37,228 --> 00:50:41,152
[SPEAKER_05]: So, in other words, person that wants to be helped, can be helped.

706
00:50:42,072 --> 00:50:42,493
[SPEAKER_05]: Otherwise,

707
00:50:45,265 --> 00:51:04,431
[SPEAKER_04]: Okay, so, what if still we do not, like, let's say, completely abandon this proposition or this idea, and maybe we have someone, let me paraphrase this, maybe we have someone who says, I do have a sense that something is off with sensuality and desire, but I don't have a clear picture as to why it is.

708
00:51:05,025 --> 00:51:07,507
[SPEAKER_04]: How would you articulate it in a way that I can see?

709
00:51:07,547 --> 00:51:11,289
[SPEAKER_04]: Because people are constantly saying, no, no, look, you know...

710
00:51:12,129 --> 00:51:20,875
[SPEAKER_05]: So, basically, what you what you're asking is, how can you show the benefit of being free from sensuality to a person who is refusing to give up sensuality?

711
00:51:21,775 --> 00:51:25,397
[SPEAKER_05]: So, the only way for them to see the benefit of it, is to give it up.

712
00:51:26,718 --> 00:51:33,002
[SPEAKER_05]: And there was a sutta that says, it's like, person at the bottom of the mountain wants to know what is the view like from the top.

713
00:51:33,944 --> 00:51:43,095
[SPEAKER_05]: I mean, I can describe it to you, but only way for you to know, is to do what I'm telling you to do, in terms of follow the instruction, and free yourself from desire, and then you'll see it for yourself.

714
00:51:43,916 --> 00:51:49,562
[SPEAKER_05]: Any other answer I give you, even if it's the right answer for me, will not be understood by you, because you're still not free from it.

715
00:51:51,485 --> 00:51:54,028
[SPEAKER_02]: It's a lot like talking to addicts, isn't it?

716
00:51:55,118 --> 00:51:57,500
[SPEAKER_05]: Well, in a way, that's, yeah, what sensuality is, yeah.

717
00:51:57,840 --> 00:52:01,904
[SPEAKER_05]: Addiction, that we know, is just the extension of the same principle of sensuality, yeah.

718
00:52:02,024 --> 00:52:15,136
[SPEAKER_02]: But you can... you can have faith in the saṅgha, for example, because this is the people that you see, you see... you guys seem pretty happy, you're, you know, you... and you... you have... Yeah, but again, that's all... again, that is all secondary, you know.

719
00:52:15,276 --> 00:52:16,877
[SPEAKER_05]: That can be a pleasing sight to you,

720
00:52:17,618 --> 00:52:24,983
[SPEAKER_05]: But fundamentally, if you're not making internal effort to restrain your mind, calm it down, understand it, makes no difference.

721
00:52:25,003 --> 00:52:26,585
[SPEAKER_05]: It's just a pleasing sight, nothing else.

722
00:52:26,965 --> 00:52:27,966
[SPEAKER_05]: Just an agreeable thing.

723
00:52:28,806 --> 00:52:31,748
[SPEAKER_00]: Because otherwise it's shifting responsibility from yourself to others.

724
00:52:31,768 --> 00:52:31,929
[SPEAKER_05]: Exactly.

725
00:52:31,949 --> 00:52:32,929
[SPEAKER_05]: That's the whole point, yeah.

726
00:52:32,969 --> 00:52:35,091
[SPEAKER_02]: But you said I'd be happy, so...

727
00:52:35,331 --> 00:52:36,532
[SPEAKER_05]: Well, the Buddha said you'll be happy.

728
00:52:36,652 --> 00:52:37,192
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, yeah, yeah.

729
00:52:37,312 --> 00:52:42,154
[SPEAKER_05]: If you, if you, if you free yourself from things that are, you know, pressuring you and making you act and controlling you.

730
00:52:42,174 --> 00:52:43,014
[SPEAKER_02]: But you have to do it yourself.

731
00:52:43,034 --> 00:52:44,235
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you've never had a complaint like this.

732
00:52:44,255 --> 00:52:48,137
[SPEAKER_02]: So, I have freed myself from all craving, and yet I'm not happy.

733
00:52:48,157 --> 00:52:49,737
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you've never heard anyone say that.

734
00:52:50,058 --> 00:52:51,598
[SPEAKER_05]: No, that's impossible, because... That's impossible.

735
00:52:51,618 --> 00:52:51,778
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah.

736
00:52:51,938 --> 00:52:52,079
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah.

737
00:52:52,099 --> 00:52:53,359
[SPEAKER_03]: They leave Buddhism, don't they?

738
00:52:56,120 --> 00:52:59,762
[SPEAKER_05]: Because if you, if you remove this source of suffering,

739
00:53:01,452 --> 00:53:04,453
[SPEAKER_05]: The gateway of suffering, there can be no suffering.

740
00:53:04,933 --> 00:53:07,774
[SPEAKER_00]: But is happiness something we should strive for, or is it peace?

741
00:53:07,794 --> 00:53:09,334
[SPEAKER_05]: It depends what you define, exactly.

742
00:53:09,794 --> 00:53:12,255
[SPEAKER_05]: Because... Is peace what's making you happy?

743
00:53:12,915 --> 00:53:21,737
[SPEAKER_05]: Is being calm, not controlled, not perturbed by the changes in your mind, by the changes... Is that what making you happy?

744
00:53:21,917 --> 00:53:24,838
[SPEAKER_05]: Or is satisfying my desires making me happy?

745
00:53:25,838 --> 00:53:27,918
[SPEAKER_05]: That's why... Oh, just do what makes you happy.

746
00:53:27,978 --> 00:53:30,119
[SPEAKER_05]: It's not a standard that you would want the world to follow.

747
00:53:30,965 --> 00:53:34,987
[SPEAKER_00]: Because happiness can actually just lead to more suffering, because we crave more happiness.

748
00:53:35,207 --> 00:53:35,487
[SPEAKER_05]: Exactly.

749
00:53:35,507 --> 00:53:39,189
[SPEAKER_05]: So, it takes wisdom to recognize what beneficial happiness is.

750
00:53:43,752 --> 00:53:51,596
[SPEAKER_03]: Basically, it seems very extreme to me, that basically, for the sake of dharma, it doesn't matter.

751
00:53:51,616 --> 00:53:54,217
[SPEAKER_03]: The whole world can die just as long as I'm in dharma.

752
00:53:55,752 --> 00:53:58,374
[SPEAKER_03]: That's the extreme example, but that's what you... What do you mean?

753
00:53:58,394 --> 00:53:58,734
[SPEAKER_05]: I mean dhamma.

754
00:53:58,754 --> 00:54:07,461
[SPEAKER_03]: I don't follow... Well, that you... you're fully enlightened, you're not going to lie, and... blow the consequences.

755
00:54:07,481 --> 00:54:09,723
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah, because they're not... not your responsibility.

756
00:54:10,463 --> 00:54:11,824
[SPEAKER_05]: Other... like, why would the world die?

757
00:54:11,844 --> 00:54:13,646
[SPEAKER_03]: Other people are my responsibility.

758
00:54:13,686 --> 00:54:14,787
[SPEAKER_03]: We're all interconnected.

759
00:54:14,827 --> 00:54:15,487
[SPEAKER_03]: We're all one.

760
00:54:15,828 --> 00:54:18,209
[SPEAKER_05]: No, that's... no, that's... that's... we disagree there, I think.

761
00:54:18,810 --> 00:54:19,270
[SPEAKER_05]: That's not.

762
00:54:19,370 --> 00:54:22,873
[SPEAKER_05]: Everybody's... as the Buddha himself said, beings are owners of their own actions.

763
00:54:22,993 --> 00:54:24,234
[SPEAKER_05]: There is no interconnectedness.

764
00:54:24,860 --> 00:54:26,985
[SPEAKER_05]: You make your own choices and you take results for them.

765
00:54:28,649 --> 00:54:29,672
[SPEAKER_01]: So, any other questions?

766
00:54:31,737 --> 00:54:32,579
[SPEAKER_05]: You went through a list, yeah?

